        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "On a second note, I'd like to ask for some clarifications.\r\n\r\nI did indeed manage to reproduce it with the following setup (on Linux in zsh):\r\n- `echo \"File original\" > file-orig`\r\n- `mkdir dir`\r\n- `ln -s file-orig dir/file-linked`\r\n\r\nThen after navigating to 'dir' directory, there is both no preview of 'file-linked' (although it is present) and it is not opened. The reason for that is that the file path is resolved as '/dir/file-orig', which is indeed not present on file system. You can verify it with `:=vim.fn.resolve('dir/file-linked')` (should print 'dir/file-orig') and `:=vim.uv.fs_stat('dir/file-linked')` (which should print `nil` and an error that file does not exist).\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, if symbolic link is created to the full path, everything works as expected. That is, after `ln -s $(pwd)'/file-orig' dir/file-linked2`, everything works as expected for 'dir/file-linked2' file.\r\n\r\nSo I'd like to ask how exactly did you create symlinks? If via linking *to* relative path, then it seems to be not a good way. Another confirmation of this is that Neovim's built-in `:edit` doesn't recognize 'dir/file-linked' but recognized 'dir/file-linked2'.",
